<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Khairpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1783 CE – 1955 CE (approx.) · capital Khairpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Talpur Mirs' state in upper Sindh, the one Talpur principality to survive the British conquest of Sindh in 1843.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Talpurs, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch-people">Baloch</a> clan, overthrew the Kalhora rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halani">Battle of Halani</a> in 1783. The victors divided Sindh, and Mir Sohrab Khan Talpur took the northern share with his capital at Khairpur.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>When the British conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 1843 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-miani">Battle of Miani</a>, Mir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-murad-khan-zand">Ali Murad Khan</a> of Khairpur had sided with them and kept his state, though he later lost land after being accused of forging a treaty. Khairpur was a Shia-ruled state with a 15-gun salute, a Sindhi-speaking population and an economy of dates, grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. The fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kot-diji">Kot Diji</a> guarded it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mir George <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-murad-khan-zand">Ali Murad Khan</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in October 1947. Khairpur was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> in 1955 and is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khairpur-state.jpg" alt="Khairpur State" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Frenchybal · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khairpur.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1783 CE – 1955 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khairpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sohrab Khan Talpur, Ali Murad Khan Talpur, George Ali Murad Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (Shia ruling house, Sunni majority)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sindhi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khairpur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1869&amp;focus=khairpur-state" aria-label="Khairpur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6486 1168 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khairpur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6806 1424L7364 1601M6806 1424L7364 1602M6806 1424L6778 1519"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6778 1519h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6806 1424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6806 1424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khairpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1869&amp;focus=khairpur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
